{"name":"Silver Birch","hex":"#d2cfc4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver birch","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A European deciduous tree, Betula pendula, characterised by white peeling bark and drooping branches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_birch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Birch (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Birch is an Irish-trained thoroughbred racehorse. He was owned through the first part of his career by Paul Barber and Des Nichols, then from 2006 by Brian Walsh from County Kildare, Ireland, a farmer and stud owner. Ridden by Robbie Power and trained by 29-year-old Gordon Elliott, Silver Birch was the winner of the 2007 John Smith's Grand National at Aintree Racecourse, run on Saturday 14 April 2007. He was Walsh and Elliott's first runner in the race."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Birch_(horse)","wikidata":"Q7515855"},{"title":"Betula pendula","blocks":[{"text":"Betula pendula, the silver birch, is a species of tree in the flowering plant family, Betulaceae. The trees can reach a height of 30 m, and have white bark that sheds regularly. It is native to Europe, Morocco, temperate Asia, Canada, and Alaska, although in southern Europe and Morocco it is only found at higher elevations. Its range extends into Siberia, China, and southwest Asia in the mountains of northern Turkey, the Caucasus, and northern Iran."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Betula_pendula_Finland.jpg/330px-Betula_pendula_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betula_pendula","wikidata":"Q156895"},{"title":"Birch","blocks":[{"text":"A birch is a thin-leaved deciduous hardwood tree of the genus Betula, in the family Betulaceae, which also includes alders, hazels, and hornbeams. It is closely related to the beech-oak family Fagaceae. The genus Betula contains 30 to 60 known taxa of which 11 are on the IUCN 2011 Red List of Threatened Species. They are typically short-lived pioneer species and are widespread in the Northern Hemisphere, particularly in northern areas of temperate climates and in boreal climates. Birch wood is used for a wide range of purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Betula_pendula_001.jpg/330px-Betula_pendula_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch","wikidata":"Q25243"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver birch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Betula_pendula0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg/500px-B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg/1280px-B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg/500px-B._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver birch has often pendulous twigs, after which the tree has received its scientific name.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AB._pendula%2C_Koivu_Birch%2C_end_of_August_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg/500px-Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg/1280px-Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg/500px-Betula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Betula pendula silver birch catkins and leaves, Childwall Woods & Fields, Merseyside","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABetula_pendula_-_Childwall_Woods_%26_Fields_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Sawfly_larvae_on_silver_birch_140701_%28Tony